=============================================================
  Cointegration Diagnostics
=============================================================

Rank condition (Granger): sigma_s(alpha_perp' Theta(1)) = 8.002e-02
  *** Not zero, but below the floor 0.20 (a convention; -rankadm sets it).
  That matrix is what makes the long-run impact
  C(1) = B_perp (Lambda_perp' Gamma B_perp)^-1 Lambda_perp'
  Theta(1) have rank M-r.  Where it degenerates the FITTED
  model denies the rank it was estimated at -- it says r and
  its parameters leave no stochastic trend.  The estimate is
  then on the edge of the region the model class allows, so
  standard errors and LR statistics do not have their usual
  distributions there.  -rankadm refuses such points; -mawarma
  makes them unreachable by construction.  See
  docs/HOMOLOGATION.md 4h.

Normalisation, share of the weight carried by the Y1 block:
  relation 1: the Y1 block carries  42.8% of the weight


=============================================================
  Joint Hypothesis Tests (Wald)
=============================================================

Error-correction term as a whole
  H0: alpha = 0
  Wald chi2(3) = 14.3568, p-value = 0.0025
  ! NOT A TEST: under H0, beta is unidentified (Davies).  Use -lrtest -bootstrap

Weak exogeneity, one variable at a time   [H0: row i of alpha = 0, chi2(1)]

invest (nabla Y2 block):
  H0: this variable does not adjust
  Wald chi2(1) = 0.0099, p-value = 0.9208
  Cannot reject H0 -> invest is weakly exogenous for B.

income (nabla Y2 block):
  H0: this variable does not adjust
  Wald chi2(1) = 0.6774, p-value = 0.4105
  Cannot reject H0 -> income is weakly exogenous for B.

cons (Y1 block):
  H0: this variable does not adjust
  Wald chi2(1) = 4.2165, p-value = 0.0400
  REJECT H0 -> cons adjusts to the disequilibrium.

Exclusion from the cointegrating relations   [H0: row i of beta_2 = 0, chi2(1)]
  only the 2 nabla Y2 variables can be excluded: beta = [I_r ; beta_2] normalises on Y1

invest:
  H0: row 1 of beta_2 = 0
  Wald chi2(1) = 4.0201, p-value = 0.0450
  REJECT H0 -> invest belongs in the long-run relation.

income:
  H0: row 2 of beta_2 = 0
  Wald chi2(1) = 185.7903, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> income belongs in the long-run relation.

All of B2 jointly:
  H0: B2 = 0 (the relation involves only the Y1 block)
  Wald chi2(2) = 76857.7820, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> the nabla Y2 block belongs in it.

Short-run dynamics   [Gamma on nabla Y, Theta on the innovations]
  ! statements about nabla Y, not about the levels: a variable can drive
    another's DIFFERENCES and still be tied to it only through ec

Joint significance of the last short-run lag, Gamma(1):
  H0: Gamma(last) = 0
  Wald chi2(9) = 21700.4566, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> the last AR lag is significant.

Joint significance of the last MA lag, Theta(1):
  H0: Theta(last) = 0
  Wald chi2(9) = 1057.2126, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> the last MA lag is significant.

All cross effects jointly:
  H0: every off-diagonal coefficient of Gamma and Theta = 0
  Wald chi2(12) = 197.2062, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> the cross structure earns its parameters.

D.invest: what the others do to it:
  H0: no other variable enters the equation of D.invest
